# Advanced Search

The default `BasicTextSearchParser` only does free-text search. Swap it out for `AdvancedSearchParser` to teach the search box about `key:value` filters. Aliases let users type whichever shorthand feels natural — `g:male`, `sex:male` and `gender:male` all parse to the same filter.

```typescript
import { AdvancedSearchParser } from 'svelte-advanced-datatable/searchParser';

type FilterType = 'gender' | 'test' | 'idAbove';

const searchParser = new AdvancedSearchParser<string, FilterType>({
	searchFilterAliases: {
		gender: ['g', 'sex'],
		test: ['t', 'tester', 'isTestUser'],
		idAbove: ['id', 'min']
	},
	additionalQueryPartParsers: [],
	enableCategoryParsing: false
});
```

The parser produces a `ParsedSearchQuery` whose `searchFilters: { type, value }[]` you interpret yourself. With `LocalDataSource` you do that in `filterFunction`; with a server-side data source you forward the filters in your request body.

```typescript
const dataSource = new LocalDataSource(exampleUserList, {
	filtering: {
		textSearchColumns: ['userName', 'firstName', 'lastName', 'mailAddress'],
		filterFunction: (item, query) => {
			for (const filter of query.searchFilters) {
				const value = filter.value.trim().toLowerCase();
				switch (filter.type) {
					case 'gender':
						if (item.gender.toLowerCase() !== value) return false;
						break;
					case 'test':
						if (item.isTestUser !== (value === 'true')) return false;
						break;
					case 'idAbove': {
						const min = Number.parseInt(value, 10);
						if (Number.isFinite(min) && item.id < min) return false;
						break;
					}
				}
			}
			return true;
		}
	}
});

const config: DataTableConfig<UserData> = {
	// ...
	searchParser,
	searchDebounceMs: 350 // give the parser a bit more breathing room
};
```

Try queries like `gender:female`, `t:true`, `id:100 gender:male`, or combine free text with a filter: `gmail gender:female`.

